(7. Reverse-engineering the vintage Intel 8087's tangent algorithm: more than CORDIC) Tj
T*
(Source: Ken Shirriff's blog | Published: 2026-09-26T09:20:56.564-07:00) Tj
T*
(Link: http://www.righto.com/2026/09/8087-tangent-cordic.html) Tj
T*
(I hope you're not tired of the 8087, because I have another article about Intel's) Tj
T*
(floating-point chip. 1 In 1980, Intel introduced the 8087, making floating-point operations) Tj
T*
(much faster in the IBM PC and other systems. In this article, I look at the algorithm behind) Tj
T*
(the chip's tangent instruction. One popular approach for trigonometric functions is an) Tj
T*
(algorithm called CORDIC. Another approach is a polynomial approximation. The 8087 combined the) Tj
T*
(two to obtain both high accuracy and high performance. The 8087 provided an enormous speedup) Tj
T*
(over the 8086 microprocessor, computing a tangent in 90 microseconds rather than 13,000) Tj
T*
(microseconds. 2 By examining the circuitry and microcode of the 8087, I can explain the) Tj
T*
(algorithm behind the tangent instruction, called FPTAN . To explore the 8087's circuitry, I) Tj
T*
(popped the lid off a chip with a chisel and created a high-resolution image with a microscope.) Tj
T*
(The microcode ROM is the large rectangular region in the center of the die, holding the 1648) Tj
T*
(micro-instructions that control the chip. The bottom half of the chip \(red box\) is the) Tj
T*
(datapath, the circuitry that performs floating-point calculations on 80-bit values. 3 A) Tj
